google / agy conformance fixtures
=================================

Witnesses for the rules in `tests/adapter_conformance.py`, which is where the
file format is documented. In short: `rc=<int>` first, an optional
`category=<expected>` (required on every `*unavailable*` file), then
`--- stdout ---` and `--- stderr ---` sections holding raw UTF-8 bytes.

stdout here is ONE JSON envelope, written at the end of the run — not a stream
and not a bare payload. Its keys are `conversation_id`, `status`, `response`,
`duration_seconds`, `num_turns`, `usage`, and, when `--json-schema` was given
and the run got far enough, `structured_output` and `json_schema`. A failed run
adds `error`. The review payload rides in `structured_output` (the copy the CLI
validated against the schema) and usually a second time, as text, in
`response`.

The loader consumes exactly one trailing newline per section, as the separator
before the next marker. Every capture below genuinely ended with a newline on
the wire, so their stdout sections end with a blank line — that blank line is
the run's own final newline, not formatting.

Provenance
----------

Every file marked CAPTURED came from a live `agy` run against agy 1.1.8 during
this task's Step 0 probe, and was then sanitized. RECOMBINED means two real
captures joined (a real stdout with a real stderr from a different run) —
stated per file, because the pair never occurred together on one run.
SYNTHESIZED would mean written by hand; **nothing here is synthesized**. The
distinction matters: a hand-written envelope proves the adapter can parse what
its author imagined, which is exactly the thing an adapter is most likely to
get wrong.

Every capture was taken from a scratch directory outside the repository, with
`--sandbox` and WITHOUT `--dangerously-skip-permissions`, using the same argv
shape `AgyAdapter.build_cmd` produces. The reviewed material is synthetic code
written for the probe, so the finding text names nothing real.

healthy.txt
    CAPTURED, whole. A completed review of a small synthetic diff
    (`svc/cache.py`): three findings, `status: SUCCESS`, rc 0, empty stderr.
    Every finding `title` is plain ASCII with no quotes or backslashes, which
    is what conformance rule 6 needs — rule 6 splices the adapter's own stderr
    signal words into a title *in the raw bytes*, and only a title that
    survives `json.dumps` unchanged appears there verbatim. The title appears
    twice in these bytes (once in `structured_output`, once inside the
    `response` text) at the same escaping depth, so a single byte replacement
    keeps both copies valid and consistent.
    STRIPPED: `conversation_id` (replaced by an all-zero UUID).

healthy_noisy_stderr.txt
    RECOMBINED: CAPTURED stdout from a second, different live review
    (`svc/token.py`, two findings) + CAPTURED stderr from the no-credentials
    run below. Rule 7's witness. The run must still classify `ok`, because the
    provider demonstrably served; the suite proves the fixture is not vacuous
    by classifying the SAME stderr with empty stdout and requiring
    `unavailable` there.
    This stdout is also the one whose `response` carries the payload TWICE at
    two different indentations — real output, and the reason extraction
    prefers `structured_output` rather than scanning the text first.
    STRIPPED: `conversation_id`; the OAuth URL (see `unavailable_auth`).

refuter_healthy.txt
    CAPTURED, whole. A live run asked for `REFUTER_CONTRACT` — the same
    invocation with the refuter schema in `--json-schema` — answering three
    findings with `confirmed`, `refuted`, `refuted`. It must parse under
    `REFUTER_CONTRACT` and must NOT parse under `REVIEW_CONTRACT`; a refuter
    response a Phase 1 caller could read as a review would read as a review
    with no findings, which is a clean bill of health.
    STRIPPED: `conversation_id`.

degraded_stderr.txt
    RECOMBINED: CAPTURED stdout (identical to `healthy.txt`) + CAPTURED stderr
    from a live auto-denied-tool run. Both halves are real; no run produced
    both, because the denied run's own stdout is the empty-response envelope
    described next.
    That denied run is the sharpest failure shape this CLI has: a prompt that
    needed a tool, in a headless run that cannot prompt for permission, so the
    call was auto-denied — and the CLI still exits **0** with **`status:
    SUCCESS`** and an **empty `response`**. The only evidence anywhere is the
    stderr line `no output produced`. It is a silent-false-all-clear generator,
    and that phrase is what stops it.
    Pairing that stderr with a valid stdout is what rule 3 needs: the run must
    classify `degraded`, NOT `unavailable`, because usable output wins on the
    availability axis only. It is also the source of the signal words rule 6
    splices into a finding title.
    The stderr is byte-faithful, including the CLI's own `jetski:` prefix and
    the em dash it writes. Both are provider constants, like the endpoint names
    in the other adapters' fixtures; rewriting them would make the fixture a
    lie about what the binary emits, and the adapter's signal matching is
    exactly what these bytes exist to pin.

degraded_timeout.txt
    CAPTURED, whole. A real `--print-timeout` expiry: rc 1, `status: ERROR`,
    `error: timeout waiting for response`, empty stderr, no payload. It carries
    no auth, model or quota wording, so it must land on `degraded` rather than
    `unavailable` — the provider was reachable and the answer was cut short,
    which buys a same-reviewer retry instead of advancing the fallback chain.
    STRIPPED: `conversation_id`.

unavailable_auth.txt
    CAPTURED, whole, from a run with `HOME` pointed at an empty scratch
    directory, so the CLI found no credentials: rc 1, `status: ERROR`,
    `error: authentication failed or timed out`, and the CLI's own
    `Authentication required` prompt on stderr. Declares `category=auth`.
    No credential of the developer's was read, written or refreshed to produce
    this: the run simply looked in a directory that had none. The category is
    asserted EXACTLY: `auth` is attempt-local, `quota` is cached provider-wide,
    and confusing the two takes a healthy provider out of every later fallback
    chain.
    STRIPPED: `conversation_id`, and the whole OAuth authorization URL —
    replaced by `https://accounts.example/oauth2/auth?REDACTED`. That URL
    carried a client id, a PKCE code challenge and a CSRF state token; none is
    a credential of the developer's, and none belongs in a public repository.
    No token was ever in these bytes — the point of the run is that there was
    none.

unavailable_quota.txt
    SYNTHESIZED envelope / BINARY-SOURCED wording. Rule 4 requires every
    adapter to witness its own provider's budget-exhaustion wording against
    its own signal table, and no live Google quota failure has been observed:
    the balance that ran out during Task 14's acceptance run was xAI's. This
    fixture is therefore NOT a capture and must not be read as one.
    The load-bearing sentence, `You have exhausted your quota on this model.`,
    is VERBATIM in the installed agy 1.1.8 binary (`strings`, alongside the
    UI's own `Quota available` / `Quota exhausted` status labels). It matches
    `exhausted your quota`, an entry added by the Task 14 audit from that same
    binary — and it is precisely the sentence this adapter's older, narrower
    table missed: that table matched `quota exceeded` and never a bare
    `quota`, deliberately, because the Google protos this binary embeds are
    dense with the word, and the binary's own sentence fell straight through
    it to `ok`. This fixture is what stops that regressing.
    What is ASSEMBLED, and is the fixture's honest weakness: the envelope
    around the sentence (`status: ERROR`, zeroed `usage`, the `Error:` stderr
    echo) follows `unavailable_auth.txt`'s real failure shape, and no quota
    run has been observed to confirm the CLI reports exhaustion in it.
    Declares `category=quota` — the only provider-wide-cacheable category, so
    the one whose misdetection costs a healthy provider every later chain.
    Deliberately does NOT contain `payment required`: that entry is marked
    speculative for this provider in `agy.py`, and a fixture witnessing a
    speculative entry would launder a guess into evidence. Replace this file
    with a real sanitized capture when a live Google quota failure occurs.

unavailable_model.txt
    CAPTURED, whole, from a run asking for a model id that does not exist.
    Declares `category=model`. stderr is EMPTY here, which is the interesting
    part: the only evidence is inside the envelope, in the harness-authored
    `error` field. The adapter reads that and never reads `response` or
    `structured_output`, which is what keeps review CONTENT from ever driving a
    classification.
    The `error` opens with `invalid model selection`, which is this CLI's
    umbrella prefix for EVERY model/effort rejection — unknown id, missing
    `--effort`, an `--effort` the model has no level for, an `--effort` that
    conflicts with an effort-suffixed id. One signal covers all four.
    The model id in this file (`gemini-9.9-nonexistent`) is deliberately not a
    real one — it is the id the CLI rejected, and rejecting it is what the
    fixture witnesses. The list of REAL model names the CLI prints in reply is
    its own public catalogue, kept verbatim. Every model id that appears in a
    passing invocation (in the tests) is one this CLI accepted live.

Sanitization
------------

These files are committed to a public repository. Every capture was checked to
contain no tokens, usernames, machine paths, home directories, repo or project
names, session ids or request ids; `conversation_id` values were replaced with
an all-zero UUID and the OAuth URL was redacted. What the captures still carry
is provider constants (the CLI's own field names, status words, error wording
and model catalogue) and usage numbers, which identify nothing.
